/* FamilyOS Launcher stylesheet.

Confirmed via a real QEMU boot test: no stylesheet was ever loaded
anywhere in this codebase, so the launcher rendered as plain default-Qt
buttons on a flat white/gray background. A later fix loaded this file
and applied a single flat green to every app card - a real second boot
test called the overall kiosk look "boring/gray" and asked for an
actual bright, playful, kid-facing visual design, in the spirit of
DoudouLinux/Qimo4kids, not just a bugfix-level color tweak. This pass
treats that as its own design effort:

- A multi-color rotation across app cards (green/amber/blue/pink/teal/
  purple - the SAME palette parental-tools/lib/render-homepage.py uses
  for the browser's homepage tiles, so the whole system reads as one
  consistent visual language, not two unrelated color schemes),
  assigned via ui/main_window.py's "cardColor" dynamic property.
- A decorative rounded border around the kiosk canvas
  (graphics/branding/familyos-logo.svg's own green, echoing the brand).
- A sparse, soft-pastel decorative background pattern (see
  graphics/branding/kiosk-background-tile.svg's own header comment for
  the seizure-risk/visual-noise reasoning behind why it's sparse and
  low-contrast-with-itself, not a busy texture) - tiled, not a single
  fixed-resolution image, since this project targets multiple screen
  resolutions.

The Parent Panel (QDialog) is DELIBERATELY NOT given this same bright
treatment - docs/Asset_Sourcing.md's original sourcing split explains
why: parent-facing icons/UI are "a normal adult-oriented settings UI,
not a kid-facing surface." It keeps its calmer cream background and
amber accent buttons, just slightly warmed/bordered so it doesn't read
as sterile-gray, without competing with or duplicating the kid-facing
screen's visual intensity.

Colors are the project's own already-established palette wherever
possible, sampled directly from graphics/branding/familyos-logo.svg
(#2E7D32 deep green, #F9A825 amber, #FFFFFF) with a handful of
additional hues in the same flat, bold-outline, high-contrast register
(no gradients, no shadows) - same visual language
docs/Asset_Sourcing.md describes borrowing from sugar-artwork for
kid-facing UI. All button text pairings below were chosen for strong
contrast against their own background (white text on the four darker/
saturated hues, dark brown text on the lighter amber) - "bright and
playful" is not the same goal as "low contrast" or "flashing/busy,"
and this stylesheet deliberately stays on the calmer side of that line:
flat fills, no animation, no dense repeating micro-patterns, no
color combinations that read as high-frequency contrast.
*/

QMainWindow {
    background-color: #FFF8E1;
    /* %KIOSK_BACKGROUND_TILE_PATH% is substituted with an absolute
       path by main.py before this stylesheet is applied - Qt QSS
       resolves a relative url() against the process's CURRENT WORKING
       DIRECTORY at the point setStyleSheet() runs (this file is loaded
       as a plain string, not via a mechanism that records its own
       location as a base path), and .xinitrc's `exec python3
       /opt/familyos/launcher/main.py` gives no guarantee about what
       that working directory actually is - a bare relative filename
       here would be fragile/likely wrong. */
    background-image: url(%KIOSK_BACKGROUND_TILE_PATH%);
}

QDialog {
    background-color: #FFF8E1;
}

QWidget#kioskCanvas {
    border: 6px solid #2E7D32;
    border-radius: 24px;
    margin: 10px;
}

QPushButton#appCard {
    border: none;
    border-radius: 24px;
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: bold;
    min-width: 140px;
    min-height: 100px;
    max-height: 240px;
    padding: 10px;
}

/* Per-card colors - see ui/main_window.py's CARD_COLOR_NAMES/"cardColor"
   property. Each has a matching :pressed shade (a darker tone of the
   same hue, not a different color) for touch/click feedback. */
QPushButton#appCard[cardColor="green"]  { background-color: #2E7D32; color: #FFFFFF; }
QPushButton#appCard[cardColor="green"]:pressed  { background-color: #245e27; }

QPushButton#appCard[cardColor="amber"]  { background-color: #F9A825; color: #3E2723; }
QPushButton#appCard[cardColor="amber"]:pressed  { background-color: #E09600; }

QPushButton#appCard[cardColor="blue"]   { background-color: #1565C0; color: #FFFFFF; }
QPushButton#appCard[cardColor="blue"]:pressed   { background-color: #0D47A1; }

QPushButton#appCard[cardColor="pink"]   { background-color: #AD1457; color: #FFFFFF; }
QPushButton#appCard[cardColor="pink"]:pressed   { background-color: #880E4F; }

QPushButton#appCard[cardColor="teal"]   { background-color: #00838F; color: #FFFFFF; }
QPushButton#appCard[cardColor="teal"]:pressed   { background-color: #006064; }

QPushButton#appCard[cardColor="purple"] { background-color: #6A1B9A; color: #FFFFFF; }
QPushButton#appCard[cardColor="purple"]:pressed { background-color: #4A148C; }

QToolButton#parentAnchor {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
    min-width: 28px;
    min-height: 28px;
}

QDialog QScrollArea {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
}

/* NOT a broader "QDialog QScrollArea QWidget { background: transparent }"
   descendant rule here: QListWidget IS a QWidget subclass nested
   inside the scroll area, and Qt's QSS specificity is computed from
   selector count, not class-hierarchy depth - a 3-simple-selector
   descendant rule can outrank the 2-simple-selector QListWidget rule
   below regardless of source order, silently undoing its intended
   white background. Left un-styled here deliberately rather than risk
   that cascade conflict for a minor cosmetic detail (the scroll area's
   own viewport showing its default background instead of true
   transparency) - QDialog's own #FFF8E1 is still the dominant color
   either way. */

QDialog QListWidget {
    background-color: #FFFFFF;
    border: 2px solid #E0D9C8;
    border-radius: 8px;
}

QDialog QPushButton {
    background-color: #F9A825;
    color: #3E2723;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 10px;
    font-size: 15px;
    font-weight: bold;
    padding: 8px 14px;
}

QDialog QPushButton:disabled {
    background-color: #E0D9C8;
    color: #9E9E9E;
}

QDialog QPushButton:pressed {
    background-color: #E09600;
}

QDialog QLabel {
    font-size: 14px;
}
